# Per-project AGENT settings. Copy to projects/<name>/.env — it is gitignored.
#
# Read AFTER the root .env (last value wins), and handed to the agent's
# container wholesale via docker-compose.yml's `env_file:` directive — every
# key here is an environment variable the agent can read. This is also where
# credential ABSENCE is made easy and obvious: each MCP server in the image
# auto-enables on credential PRESENCE, so a project that should only reach
# GitLab blanks every other credential explicitly rather than leaving them
# to whatever the root .env happened to set.
#
# Same parser rule as the root .env: comments on their OWN lines, never
# inline after a value.
#
# No SYMPHONY_* key belongs in this file — those are the orchestrator's,
# read from projects/<name>/symphony.env, and never handed to this
# container. `symphony check` warns if one leaks in here.
#
# There is no PROJECT_SLUG here either: ./symphony derives it from this
# directory's own name (projects/<name>/) and passes it to compose itself, so
# a project's slug is spelled in exactly one place — the directory it lives
# in.

# === The agent's GitLab credential ===========================================
# A project (or group) access token, DEVELOPER role, scopes `api` +
# `write_repository` — NOT your personal PAT. A personal PAT carries your
# whole GitLab identity and nothing in this container can claw that back,
# because the token IS the authority. See the README's "The token's scope
# is the boundary".
GITLAB_PAT=

# === Credential absence removes capability ===================================
# Each MCP server auto-enables the moment its full credential set is present,
# so an EXPLICIT BLANK is how you say "this server is not here for this
# project" rather than "disabled" — the server is never wired into
# opencode.json at all. A key merely left OUT of this file, by contrast, is
# INHERITED from the root .env: leaving BITBUCKET_PAT out here would silently
# give this project whatever the shared file has. Blank it instead.
BITBUCKET_PAT=
JIRA_PAT=
JFROG_PAT=
CONFLUENCE_PAT=
MFILES_PAT=

# === Two gates, two planes ====================================================
# GIT_REMOTE_ALLOWLIST is enforced by the git-guard PATH shim (the git
# plane); GITLAB_WRITE_PROJECTS is enforced by the MCP write-tool gate (the
# API plane). Different processes, so nothing at runtime makes them agree
# with each other or with this project's actual GitLab remote — that
# cross-check is what `symphony check` is for. Get all three pointing at the
# same project by hand.
#
# Both gates ship OFF, which is stage 0 of the rollout described in the
# README's "Your first run": the agent can commit locally and CANNOT push,
# so a first run carries zero remote risk while answering the only question
# that matters first — does your model hold an unattended multi-turn loop at
# all, or does it derail at turn four? Flip both to 1, against a sandbox
# project, once that answer is yes. A template that shipped them ON would
# quietly skip that stage for anyone who copied it without reading.
ALLOW_REMOTE_GIT=0
GIT_REMOTE_ALLOWLIST=gitlab.internal.example/my-group/my-project
ALLOW_GITLAB_WRITE=0
GITLAB_WRITE_PROJECTS=my-group/my-project

# === Optional: debug publisher ================================================
# Only read by `symphony up --publish` (docker/docker-compose.publish.yml).
# Host port for the opencode server and the base of the derived opencode-pty
# viewer port (1<this>). Unset, it defaults the same place OPENCODE_INTERNAL_PORT
# does. Give every project you might publish at once a distinct value so their
# loopback bindings don't collide.
# OPENCODE_PORT=4096
